hered"}],"gloss":"withered"},{"surface":"பசியர்","parts":[{"text":"பசி","lemma":"பசி","gloss":"hunger"},{"text":"அர்","lemma":"அர்","gloss":"they who have"}],"gloss":"hungry ones"},{"surface":"ஆகிப்","parts":[{"text":"ஆகி","lemma":"ஆகு","gloss":"having become"},{"text":"ப்","lemma":"ப்","gloss":"consonant"}],"gloss":"having become"},{"surface":"பிறர்","parts":[{"text":"பிறர்","lemma":"பிறர்","gloss":"others"}],"gloss":"others"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"நாடுபடு","parts":[{"text":"நாடு","lemma":"நாடு","gloss":"country"},{"text":"படு","lemma":"படு","gloss":"seeking"}],"gloss":"seeking other countries"},{"surface":"செலவினர்","parts":[{"text":"செலவு","lemma":"செலவு","gloss":"journey"},{"text":"இனர்","lemma":"இனர்","gloss":"they who have"}],"gloss":"those who journey"},{"surface":"ஆயினர்","parts":[{"text":"ஆயினர்","lemma":"ஆகு","gloss":"they became"}],"gloss":"they became"},{"surface":"இனியே","parts":[{"text":"இனி","lemma":"இனி","gloss":"now"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"indeed"}],"gloss":"now indeed"}]}],"literal":"Swaying-gait horses and male elephants and chariots, / unfading fresh wealth country and towns, / to bards unstinting Ay Andiran, / with curve-prominent waist small-bracelet women, / pitiless one called Kalan (Death) driving away, / reached the world of the celestials, so saying, / inside the hollowed cave, the gaping-mouthed owl, / pointing 'gather!' thus calling the dead, / in one corner of the cactus-filled cremation ground staying, / as the bright fire consumed, his body perished. / Dimmed-eyed, not seeing their patron, / with their clamorous retinue, helplessly despairing, the poets, / having become withered and hungry, / became those who journey seeking other countries now indeed.","translation":"Ay Andiran, who unstintingly gave swaying-paced horses, male elephants, chariots, and towns of unfading, fertile lands to bards, has been driven away by pitiless Death to the world of the celestials. In a corner of the cactus-filled cremation ground, where the gaping-mouthed owl from its hollowed cave calls out to the dead, 'Point and gather!', his body has perished, consumed by the bright fire. Now, unable to see their patron, poets with dimmed eyes and clamorous families have fallen into helpless despair; withered by hunger, they have now set out on journeys to other lands.","modern":"அசைந்து நடக்கும் குதிரைகளையும், ஆண் யானைகளையும், தேர்களையும், வளம் குறையாத நாடுகளையும் ஊர்களையும் பாடி வரும் இரவலர்களுக்கு வாரி வழங்கிய வள்ளல் ஆய் அண்டிரன். வளைந்த இடுப்பையும் சிறிய வளையல்களையும் அணிந்த தன் மனைவியருடன், இரக்கமற்ற காலன் என்னும் எமன் கொண்டு செலுத்த, அவன் தேவர் உலகத்தை அடைந்தான். பொந்துகளை உடைய மரப்பொந்துகளில் வாழும், பிளந்த வாயைக் கொண்ட கூகை, 'சுட்டுக் குவிப்பீராக' என்று இறந்தவர்களைக் கூவி அழைக்கும் கள்ளிச் செடிகள் நிறைந்த சுடுகாட்டின் ஒரு பக்கத்தில், பிரகாசமான நெருப்பு தின்ன அவனது உடல் அழிந்துபோனது. தங்களைக் காக்கும் வள்ளலைக் காணாமல், ஒளி இழந்த கண்களை உடையவர்களாய், தங்களின் ஆரவாரம் மிக்க சுற்றத்தாருடன் செய்வதறியாது திகைத்து, பசியால் வாடி, புலவர்கள் இப்போது பிற நாடுகளை நோக்கிச் செல்பவர்கள் ஆயினர்.","explanation":"The poet Kuttuvan Kiranar is lamenting the death of the great patron chieftain Ay Andiran. The poem contrasts the past glory of Ay, who generously gifted horses, elephants, and fertile lands to bards, with the grim reality of his cremation. He has been taken by pitiless Death to the upper world. His body has been cremated in a desolate, cactus-filled wasteland where owls screech. Consequently, the poets who depended on him are left helpless, starving, and forced to wander to other countries in search of new patrons. This poem belongs to the Potuviyal tinai and Kaiyarunilai (lamentation) theme.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.5],[0.5,0.7],[0.7,0.8],[0.8,1.5],[1.5,1.8],[1.8,2.7],[2.7,2.8],[2.8,3.3],[3.3,3.6],[3.6,5.1],[5.1,5.5],[5.5,5.7],[5.7,6.3],[6.3,7.5],[7.5,7.9],[7.9,8.6],[8.6,9.2],[9.2,10.4],[10.4,10.6],[10.6,10.8],[10.8,11.2],[11.2,12.3],[12.3,12.3],[12.3,12.7],[12.7,13.6],[13.6,14.4],[